Boost 1.92.0's Boost.Cobalt gives a coroutine frame storage aligned to std::max_align_t only: its
promises' operator new (promise_memory_resource_base in boost/cobalt/this_coro.hpp, Cobalt's
promise, task, generator and detached) stores the memory resource in the first
sizeof(std::max_align_t) bytes of the block and returns the address after them. On MSVC
std::max_align_t is 8 bytes and __STDCPP_DEFAULT_NEW_ALIGNMENT__ is 16, so the frame lies 8 bytes
off a 16-byte boundary, though an operator new without std::align_val_t must return storage
aligned to 16 there. MSVC's optimizer stores into the frame with aligned SSE instructions (movaps),
which fault: webcpp::hash's async_cobalt test exited with 0xC0000005 on MSVC 14.3 x64 release in
every run (the fault at movaps [frame+0x6D90] with the frame at 8 mod 16). The upstream fix is
https://github.com/boostorg/cobalt/commit/d67080b ("Added MSVC max_align workaround"), which adds
boost::cobalt::coroutine_align and coroutine_align_t to config.hpp and uses them in every coroutine
allocation of Cobalt. This patch takes the same names and the same places, MSVC's values (16, and
a 16-byte type aligned to 16) and upstream's change to the unsized branch of detail/main.hpp, in
one other form: the header before a frame is counted in sizeof(coroutine_align_t), where upstream
counts it in coroutine_align. Off MSVC, coroutine_align is alignof(std::max_align_t) and
coroutine_align_t is std::max_align_t, so every size, offset and alignment is Boost 1.92.0's (the
alignment the patch passes is the one the resources took by default), and only MSVC's build
changes; upstream's form would also shrink the header off MSVC, from 32 to 16 bytes on x86-64 and
arm64 Linux and on WebAssembly. Remove this file once the Boost that
tools/ci/actions/boost/install.sh pins contains the upstream fix: install.sh then fails, saying the
patch does not apply.

